--- AFTER: the three corrected places ---
$ sed -n "56,69p" Caddyfile
	# Kept conversations. `/s/<id>` is a page the engine renders and it does not live under
	# `/api`, so without this block the catch-all below hands the path to the file server and
	# every share link answers 404 — which is what the shipped configuration did (A99).
	#
	# The difference from the block above is deliberate: no `header_up Host`, so Caddy passes the
	# Host it was reached on through to the engine, which checks it and hands it to the page as
	# `shareBase`. What the page *does* with it is nothing — every URL it could need is relative,
	# and the page renders no links of its own (A217). The link a reader copies is built by the
	# front ends instead: the web interface from the origin the browser is reading at, with the
	# engine's reported base as its fallback (`web/app.js`), and the desktop app from that reported
	# base (`ChatController.shareLink(for:)`), which `--share-base` sets.
	handle /s/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:7789
	}

$ sed -n "449,454p;662,668p" Sources/ChatBotsCore/APIServer.swift
    ///
    /// Reflecting the header is how the engine learns the origin that actually served the page — the
    /// value it passes to the share page as `shareBase`, which the page does not read yet (A217). The
    /// value is client-supplied either way: anything carrying a path, a userinfo `@`, whitespace or a
    /// character a host or port cannot contain is refused rather than interpolated. A refused value
    /// falls back to the configured base.
   ...
